# WI-15 — label-state probes

What this file is: the recorded, read-only probes that establish the shape of the issue-label
state the harness reads, so the `readIssueLabels` parse is written against **observed** gh output
rather than an assumption. **Every command in Part 1 is read-only.** Part 2 copies the
pre-grilling record verbatim, and that record contains outward-facing `gh issue edit
… --remove-label` calls — each run on a **non-mutating case**, and recorded here rather than
re-run (Part 2 says why). Later probes are appended by the task that runs them, each with its own
command, exit code and verbatim output; where the source record summarizes instead of printing,
the gap is labelled rather than filled in.

The file holds **nine** probe blocks in **two** sets:

- **Part 1 — the T2 shape probes: four blocks (1, 2, 3, 3b).** They establish the JSON *shape*
  the `readIssueLabels` parse is written against, and were run during T2.
- **Part 2 — the pre-grilling probes: five blocks.** They were run before grilling and are
  **copied verbatim** from `docs/work/WI-15/prd.md` § "Facts verified before grilling
  (2026-09-23)"; none was re-run for this file.

**Which "five" the specification means.** The specification's evidence boundary names "the five
recorded probe commands" — that phrase refers to the **pre-grilling** set (Part 2). The T2 shape
probes are four, not five, and they are the parse-shape evidence, a different thing.

The confounder this file exists to remove: the issue this work item is about was caused by the
harness interpreting a subprocess's *text* instead of reading its *state* — so the shape of the
state is probed first, before any parse is written against it.

---

# Part 1 — T2 shape probes (2026-09-23)

## Probe 1 — the plan's owner-approved shape probe (fixture repo)

Command:

```
gh issue list --repo manjula25/loop-fixtures-py --state all --limit 5 --json number,labels
```

Exit code: `0`

Verbatim stdout:

```
[{"labels":[],"number":54},{"labels":[],"number":52},{"labels":[],"number":49},{"labels":[],"number":48},{"labels":[],"number":45}]
```

What it establishes: gh's `--json labels` field puts the labels under a `labels` **array key** per
issue (so `JSON.parse(stdout)` yields an array of issues, each with a `labels` array). What it
does **not** establish: the shape of an array *element* — every array here is empty, so this probe
alone cannot distinguish `{name: string}` objects from bare strings. It was recorded as
inconclusive on that point rather than read as if it had settled it; probes 3 and 3b below supply
the missing element shape. **This is the fork the plan flagged, reached honestly.**

## Probe 2 — is any issue in the fixture repo even labelled?

Command:

```
gh issue list --repo manjula25/loop-fixtures-py --state all --limit 100 --json number,labels
```

Exit code: `0`

Verbatim stdout (re-run by T4, 2026-09-24 — one JSON line, unmodified, no elision):

```
[{"labels":[],"number":54},{"labels":[],"number":52},{"labels":[],"number":49},{"labels":[],"number":48},{"labels":[],"number":45},{"labels":[],"number":44},{"labels":[],"number":41},{"labels":[],"number":40},{"labels":[],"number":37},{"labels":[],"number":36},{"labels":[],"number":32},{"labels":[],"number":31},{"labels":[],"number":30},{"labels":[],"number":29},{"labels":[],"number":26},{"labels":[],"number":23},{"labels":[],"number":22},{"labels":[],"number":20},{"labels":[],"number":18},{"labels":[],"number":14},{"labels":[],"number":10},{"labels":[],"number":3},{"labels":[],"number":2},{"labels":[],"number":1}]
```

What it establishes: no issue in the synthetic fixture repo wears a label — the array holds 24
issue objects and every `labels` array in it is empty, so the element shape cannot be observed
there at any `--limit`. Probe 1's emptiness is a property of the fixture data, not of gh's
serialisation — which is why the element shape had to be observed elsewhere.

**Correction (T4, 2026-09-24).** This block previously displayed a *filtered* result — `[]` under
the heading "Verbatim result" plus the line `total issues: 24` — without showing the filter,
which falls below the standard probes 1/3/3b set. The command above was therefore re-run against
the same public synthetic fixture repo (read-only) and its unmodified stdout is recorded here.
Nothing else in this block's conclusion changed: the repo still carries no labelled issue.

## Probe 3 — the element shape, on a public repo with a labelled issue

Command (same `--json labels` field as probe 1, on a public repository):

```
gh issue list --repo cli/cli --state all --limit 3 --json number,labels
```

Exit code: `0`

Verbatim stdout:

```
[{"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDODKw3uc7QD3p7","name":"needs-triage","description":"needs to be reviewed","color":"D6393F"}],"number":14512},{"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDODKw3uc7QD3p7","name":"needs-triage","description":"needs to be reviewed","color":"D6393F"}],"number":14511},{"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDODKw3uc7QD3p7","name":"needs-triage","description":"needs to be reviewed","color":"D6393F"}],"number":14510}]
```

What it establishes: a `labels` array element is an **object carrying `name`** (alongside `id`,
`description`, `color`) — not a bare string. The plan's expected case holds, so the parse is
`.labels.map((l) => l.name)`; `.map(String)` would have been wrong here (it would yield
`"[object Object]"` and every membership check would fail).

## Probe 3b — the wiring's own subcommand, on a labelled issue

The wiring calls `gh issue view <n> --json labels` (not `gh issue list`), so the element shape is
confirmed on that subcommand too. The probe adds `--repo cli/cli` only to point that same
subcommand at a repository whose issue carries a label; the wiring itself omits `--repo` and is
scoped by the target repo's cwd instead, so this is the wiring's subcommand and `--json` field,
not its literal argv.

```
gh issue view 14512 --repo cli/cli --json labels
```

Exit code: `0`

Verbatim stdout:

```
{"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDODKw3uc7QD3p7","name":"needs-triage","description":"needs to be reviewed","color":"D6393F"}]}
```

What it establishes: `issue view --json labels` returns the same top-level `{"labels":[...]}`
object with the same element shape, so `JSON.parse(raw) as { labels: readonly { name: string }[] }`
followed by `.map((l) => l.name)` matches observed output. (Consistent with the shape already
recorded in `docs/work/WI-15/prd.md`: `gh issue view 54 --json labels,state` →
`{"labels":[],"state":"CLOSED"}`, exit 0 — that record confirms the top-level key, this probe
confirms the element.)

## Authorization status of probes 3 and 3b (recorded honestly, T4)

The implementation plan authorized **one** read-only probe — T2 step 1, on the fixture repo
`manjula25/loop-fixtures-py`. That probe could not determine the JSON **element** shape: no issue
in the fixture repo carries a label (probe 2 above), so every `labels` array it returned was
empty. The implementer therefore extended the probing to `cli/cli`, a public third-party
repository, for probes 3 and 3b, in order to read an issue that actually wears a label.

This deviation was **not pre-authorized**: the plan named no repository other than the fixture
repo, and this file does not claim an authorization that did not exist. What can be said plainly
is what was true of the two probes themselves — both were read-only (`gh issue list` /
`gh issue view`), on a public, non-client repository, so the confidentiality gate (hard
constraint 3) is not engaged. The controller accepted the deviation after the fact and surfaced
it to the owner. It is recorded here rather than left implicit.

